## Solution

We compute the limit

$$L = \lim_{n\to+\infty} n\int_{0}^{\pi/2} x\, f(x)\,\cos^n x\, dx,\qquad f\in C([0,\pi/2]).$$

### Step 1. Substitution

Set $t = \sqrt{n}\,x$, i.e. $x = t/\sqrt{n}$, $dx = dt/\sqrt{n}$. Then

$$n\int_{0}^{\pi/2} x\,f(x)\cos^n x\,dx
= \int_{0}^{\sqrt{n}\,\pi/2} t\, f\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{n}}\right)\cos^n\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{n}}\right) dt.$$

Denote the integrand by $g_n(t) = t\,f(t/\sqrt{n})\,\cos^n(t/\sqrt{n})$ on $[0,\sqrt{n}\,\pi/2]$ and $0$ on $(\sqrt{n}\,\pi/2,\infty)$.

### Step 2. Pointwise limit

For each fixed $t\ge 0$ (and $n$ large enough that $t/\sqrt{n}<\pi/2$):

- By continuity of $f$: $f(t/\sqrt{n})\to f(0)$.
- Using $\ln\cos u = -u^2/2 + O(u^4)$ as $u\to 0$,

$$n\ln\cos\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{n}}\right) = n\!\left(-\tfrac{t^2}{2n}+O(n^{-2})\right) \to -\tfrac{t^2}{2},$$

so $\cos^n(t/\sqrt{n})\to e^{-t^2/2}$.

Hence $g_n(t)\to t\,f(0)\,e^{-t^2/2}$ pointwise on $[0,\infty)$.

### Step 3. Dominating function

For $u\in[0,\pi/2)$, since $\tan u\ge u$, we have $\ln\cos u = -\int_0^u \tan s\,ds \le -\int_0^u s\,ds = -u^2/2$, hence

$$\cos u \le e^{-u^2/2}.$$

Therefore, for $t\in[0,\sqrt{n}\,\pi/2]$,

$$\cos^n\!\left(\tfrac{t}{\sqrt{n}}\right)\le e^{-t^2/2}.$$

With $M=\max_{[0,\pi/2]}|f|$, we get

$$|g_n(t)|\le M\,t\,e^{-t^2/2}\in L^1([0,\infty)),$$

and $g_n(t)=0$ for $t>\sqrt{n}\,\pi/2$. So dominated convergence applies.

### Step 4. Evaluate the limit

$$L = f(0)\int_0^\infty t\,e^{-t^2/2}\,dt = f(0)\left[-e^{-t^2/2}\right]_0^\infty = f(0)\cdot(0-(-1)) = f(0).$$

### Result

$$\boxed{\lim_{n\to+\infty}n\int_{0}^{\pi/2} x\,f(x)\cos^n x\,dx = f(0).}$$

